<h2>The discovery and the theory</h2>
<p>On 7 September 2011, police opened a locked house to find the bodies of Mithilesh Kumari and her minor son Bhupinder Singh. The post-mortem indicated death by strangulation five to seven days earlier — placing the likely date around the time the two were last seen alive. Suspicion settled on the deceased woman&rsquo;s stepdaughter. The prosecution assembled a familiar circumstantial scaffold: last-seen evidence placing the accused near the deceased, a thumb injury for which she took treatment on 1 September 2011, a lock allegedly used to secure the death scene, call records, a property-related motive, and a DNA report matching her blood to stains on a trunk inside the house.</p>

<h2>The audit of each circumstance</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court examined each strand and found each one frayed:</p>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Circumstance</th><th>Why it failed</th></tr>
<tr><td>Last-seen testimony</td><td>The witness&rsquo;s account improved upon his earlier statement; the corroborating taxi driver and hotel worker lacked credibility, with no hotel records and no explanation of why their statements came to be recorded</td></tr>
<tr><td>The lock on the house</td><td>Identified on the handle rather than the latch; no witness conclusively connected the recovered lock to the lock actually securing the scene</td></tr>
<tr><td>DNA match on the trunk</td><td>The accused had visited the house before; her blood appeared nowhere on the victims&rsquo; clothing or the strangulation material; the lone intact sample amid degraded ones was never explained</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call detail records</td><td>Tower location data that could have placed the parties together was missing; the complete CDR was never exhibited</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motive</td><td>No evidence proved any property bequest to the stepbrother, undercutting the alleged grievance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medical timeline</td><td>Autopsy findings permitted death after 1 September 2011, leaving the prosecution&rsquo;s chronology unresolved</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The double burden in appeals against acquittal</h2>
<p>Two settled doctrines converged. First, in a case resting wholly on circumstantial evidence, the established circumstances must form a complete chain pointing only to the accused and excluding every reasonable hypothesis of innocence. Second, where the High Court has acquitted, the presumption of innocence stands reinforced, and the Supreme Court will not substitute its own view unless the acquittal is perverse or impossible on the record. Measured against either doctrine — let alone both — the State&rsquo;s appeal could not succeed, and the acquittal recorded by the High Court was affirmed.</p>

<h2>Lessons for investigation and defence</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Forensics need context.</strong> A DNA match answers &ldquo;whose?&rdquo;, not &ldquo;when?&rdquo; or &ldquo;how?&rdquo;. Prior legitimate access to the premises can neutralise a match unless the prosecution ties the sample to the crime itself.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Exhibit the full record.</strong> Partial call records without tower data invite adverse inference rather than supply corroboration.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Recoveries must connect.</strong> A lock, a weapon or a garment proves little unless a witness links the specific object to the specific scene.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Statements age poorly.</strong> Improvements between a witness&rsquo;s first statement and the deposition are the most common solvent of last-seen evidence.</div>
</div>

<div class="note"><p>Practice pointer: in defending a circumstantial case, cross-examination aimed at the <em>gaps between</em> circumstances — dates, access, chain of custody of forensic samples — is usually more productive than attacking any single circumstance head-on. This judgment shows an entire prosecution dissolving at the joints rather than at any one link.</p></div>

<h2>The broader signal</h2>
<p>Fifteen years separated the occurrence from the final word, a reminder of how long circumstantial murder trials and their appeals can run. For the prosecution, the case underscores that charges should follow evidence that can survive appellate scrutiny, not merely suspicion strong enough to charge. For the defence, it reaffirms that an acquittal well reasoned at the High Court stage is a formidable shield in the Supreme Court.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
